   "date": "2019-02-04T22:59:53Z",
   "subject": "Re: Standardized item residuals",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">You could try the residuals() function in mirt. There&#39;s quite a few statistics related to that. And yes M2 allows you to see the residuals cov matrix too, just ask for it from the function arguments. <div><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Phil</div></div></div></div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Mon, Feb 4, 2019 at 5:55 PM Eric Burkholder &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">eric1...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I see, that clears things up. Is there a way to explicitly investigate the &quot;bivariate item fit&quot; that he refers to? I know that the M2 function does a test of absolute goodness of fit considering both univariate and bivariate item fits, but can we see this explicitly anywhere?<div><br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Mon, Feb 4, 2019 at 2:10 PM Phil Chalmers &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">rphilip....@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\">That&#39;s not what Templin is referring to, unfortunately. I believe his notes pertain to the classical X2 statistics, which are generally less useful these days. These require computing the proportion of responses given a *binned* theta, and determining a goodness-of-fit statistics based on these binned data terms. This is available in the itemfit() function, in addition to having the plots generated for the same data. But, note that these are generally not very good fit statistics unless the test length is extremely long; otherwise, they are quite liberal representations of misfit. <div><br></div><div>library(mirt)</div><div>dat &lt;- expand.table(LSAT7)</div><div>mod &lt;- mirt(dat, 1)</div><div>itemfit(mod, fit_stats = &#39;X2&#39;, method = &#39;ML&#39;)<br></div><div>itemfit(mod, empirical.plot = 1, method = &#39;ML&#39;)</div><div><div><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Phil</div></div></div></div><br></div></div></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Mon, Feb 4, 2019 at 4:48 PM Eric Burkholder &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">eric1...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><span style=\"font-size:small\">Nevermind, I found what I was looking for:</span><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><a href=\"https://jonathantemplin.com/teaching/#1546295939991-6ff3016d-db4f\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://jonathantemplin.com/teaching/%231546295939991-6ff3016d-db4f&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792473021497000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2862IUQ87pB5-NftLvA-TP\">Templin&#39;s notes on IRT</a> recommend looking at standardized residuals of an item to examine fit. There is a way to do this in the mirt framework:</div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:rgb(136,0,0)\">##Dichotomous item response data: 1500 responses on 47 multiple-choice items marked as right (1) or wrong (0)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br></span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">ItemDat</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">read</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">csv</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,136,0)\">&quot;FMCE_Dat.csv&quot;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:rgb(136,0,0)\">##Fit a unidimensional 2PL model</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>mod1 </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> mirt</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">ItemDat</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,136,0)\">&#39;2PL&#39;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:rgb(136,0,0)\">##Extract information for item 1 and abilities</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>extr</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1.1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> extract</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">item</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">mod1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br></span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Theta</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> fscores</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">mod1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)[,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:rgb(136,0,0)\">##Compute standard error:</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>SE</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1.1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> sqrt</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">probtrace</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">extr</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1.1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Theta</span></font><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)[,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">2</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]*</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">probtrace</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">extr</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1.1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Theta</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)[</span><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]/</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">length</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">probtrace</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">extr</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1.1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Theta</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)[,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]))</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:rgb(136,0,0)\">##Compute standard residuals:</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br><br>SR</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1.1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">ItemDat</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">[,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">expected</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">item</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">extr</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1.1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Theta</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">))/</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">SE</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1.1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:rgb(136,0,0)\">##Plot standardized residuals to examine heteroskedacitiy:</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br><br>plot</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Theta</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">SR</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1.1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br></span></font><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br><br><br></span></div></code></div><br><br></div><br>On Monday, February 4, 2019 at 12:55:00 PM UTC-8, Eric Burkholder wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hello,<div><br></div><div>I am wondering if there is a way to return the standardized item residuals for a mirt model. That is, (P(\\theta) - E[P(\\theta)])/SE(\\theta). All of these quantities can be plotted in item plot, but I want to return the values so I can look at the distri